H1('Abstract')
P(f'Sorbents for direct air capture and flue-gas treatment must bind CO_{{2}} far more strongly than N_{{2}} at very low CO_{{2}} partial pressure. Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are attractive candidates, but the experimentally reported COF landscape has not been mapped systematically in this dilute limit, and it is unclear whether structure-based machine learning (ML) offers advantages over simpler models for this task. Here we compute infinite-dilution CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} Henry selectivities and CO_{{2}} adsorption enthalpies for {n} quality-controlled frameworks from the CURATED-COFs database by Widom test-particle insertion, quantify the intrinsic CO_{{2}} and N_{{2}} affinity of common linkage motifs with counterpoise-corrected PBE-D3(BJ) cluster calculations, and benchmark a SchNet graph neural network against random-forest and multilayer-perceptron models. Selectivity spans {f1(S.min())}–{S.max():.0f} (median {f1(S.median())}) and is governed mainly by pore confinement (Spearman ρ = {rho_lcd:.2f} with the largest cavity diameter) rather than by linkage chemistry; only {nS10} frameworks ({100*nS10/n:.0f}%) exceed a selectivity of 10. A descriptor-based random forest predicts log-selectivity with a cross-validated mean absolute error of {f3(rf["MAE"])} (R^{{2}} = {f2(rf["R2"])}){", outperforming the graph network (MAE " + f3(sch["MAE"]) + ")" if sch else ""}. Trained on only {ml["n_train_screen"]} diversity-selected frameworks, it recovers {eff["RF_screen"]["recall_at_20pct"]*100:.0f}% of the top-5% performers among the remaining {ml["n_test_screen"]} after simulating 20% of them. Simulated high-pressure CO_{{2}} capacities agree with experiment to within {val["hp_mape"]:.0f}% on average, whereas low-pressure uptake of nitrogen-rich frameworks is underestimated, pointing to the limits of generic force fields for the strongest binding sites. All structures, inputs, scripts and results are openly available.')
P('**Keywords:** covalent organic frameworks; CO_{2} capture; direct air capture; high-throughput screening; Widom insertion; density functional theory; machine learning; graph neural networks')

# ======================= INTRODUCTION =======================
H1('Introduction')
P(f'Limiting global warming in line with the Paris Agreement requires both deep emission cuts and the removal of CO_{{2}} that has already been emitted{cite("ipcc2022")}. Capture from flue gas (roughly 10–15% CO_{{2}}) and direct air capture (DAC, about 420 ppm CO_{{2}}) both rely on sorbents that bind CO_{{2}} much more strongly than N_{{2}}, the dominant competing gas{cite("sanzperez2016", "farmahini2021")}. At DAC conditions the CO_{{2}} partial pressure is so low that adsorption takes place in the Henry regime, where performance is set by the strength and density of the strongest binding sites. The infinite-dilution (Henry) selectivity and the zero-coverage heat of adsorption are therefore natural first-pass metrics for ranking materials before more expensive process-level evaluation{cite("lin2012", "huck2014", "farmahini2021")}.')
P(f'Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are crystalline porous polymers built from light elements and joined by strong covalent bonds{cite("cote2005", "elkaderi2007", "diercks2017", "geng2020")}. Their linkage chemistry (boroxine, boronate ester, imine, β-ketoenamine, triazine, azine, hydrazone and others), pore size and pore-wall functionality can be tuned largely independently, and many are chemically robust. This makes them attractive alternatives to zeolites and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), whose performance can be compromised by hydrolytic instability or competitive water adsorption{cite("boyd2019")}. Amine-functionalised COFs have recently been shown to capture CO_{{2}} from open air over many cycles{cite("zhou2024")}, and more than a thousand COF structures have now been reported, of which curated, computation-ready collections are available{cite("tong2018", "ongari2019", "ongari2020")}.')
P(f'Computational screening has already been used to rank COFs for carbon capture. Deeg et al. evaluated about 69,000 hypothetical COFs by parasitic energy using force-field simulations with tuned charge-equilibration charges{cite("deeg2020")}, and Mercado et al. constructed a large in-silico COF database for gas storage{cite("mercado2018")}. For MOFs, data-driven workflows that combine simulation with ML have identified frameworks that were subsequently synthesised and tested{cite("boyd2019", "moosavi2020", "jablonka2020")}. Descriptor-based models such as random forests remain strong baselines in chemical property prediction, for example in QSAR models of compound toxicity built on computed molecular descriptors{cite("khanal2026qsar")}. Graph neural networks (GNNs) such as CGCNN and SchNet, which learn directly from atomic structure, are attractive because they avoid hand-crafted descriptors{cite("butler2018", "xie2018", "schutt2018", "rosen2021")}.')
P('Three gaps motivate the present work. First, most COF screening studies have used hypothetical structures; the experimentally reported COFs, which are the materials that can be made today, have received less systematic attention for CO_{2}/N_{2} selectivity in the dilute limit. Second, it has not been established whether structure-only GNNs outperform descriptor-based models for adsorption in porous frameworks, where performance depends on the void space as much as on the atoms that bound it. Third, ML screening studies rarely quantify how much simulation is actually saved, or whether model uncertainty identifies unreliable predictions.')
P(f'Here we address these gaps using the experimentally reported frameworks of the CURATED-COFs database. We (i) compute infinite-dilution CO_{{2}} and N_{{2}} Henry coefficients, Henry selectivities and CO_{{2}} adsorption enthalpies for every framework by Widom test-particle insertion; (ii) quantify the intrinsic CO_{{2}} and N_{{2}} affinity of five common linkage motifs with counterpoise-corrected, dispersion-corrected density functional theory (DFT); (iii) benchmark a SchNet GNN against random-forest (RF) and multilayer-perceptron (MLP) models built on geometric and chemical descriptors; (iv) test a realistic screening scenario in which models trained on 100 diversity-selected COFs rank the remainder; and (v) compare simulated adsorption with experimental data. All data, inputs and code are released openly.')

# ======================= METHODS =======================
H1('Methods')
H2('Structures and quality control')
P(f'All 874 frameworks listed in cof-frameworks.csv of the CURATED-COFs database (ref. {cite("ongari2019", "ongari2020")[2:-1]}) were used as deposited. The 110 additional CIF files in the repository are listed in cof-discarded.csv (duplicates, unobserved stacking or interpenetration variants) and were not considered. Three frameworks with more than 3,000 atoms per unit cell were excluded for computational cost. Framework partial charges were assigned by the extended charge-equilibration method (EQeq; λ = 1.2, hydrogen electron affinity −2 eV, Ewald summation){cite("wilmer2012")}, and the small residual net charge from rounding was spread uniformly. EQeq did not converge for one framework, and the Widom calculations of four others did not complete, leaving {qc["n_widom"]} frameworks with adsorption data. Of these, frameworks were removed if EQeq produced unphysical charges (|q| > 2.5 e; {qc["eqeq_fail"]} frameworks), if they contained alkali-metal counter-ions (Li^{{+}}, Na^{{+}}; {qc["alkali"]} frameworks), for which point-charge/Lennard-Jones force fields without polarisation give unphysically strong binding, or if they had no CO_{{2}}-accessible surface (probe radius 1.65 Å; {qc["inaccessible"]} frameworks). The final screening set contains {n} frameworks.')
H2('Descriptors and diversity selection')
P(f'Geometric descriptors were computed with Zeo++ in high-accuracy mode{cite("willems2012")}: largest included sphere (LCD), largest free sphere (pore-limiting diameter, PLD), accessible surface area (probe radius 1.65 Å, 2,000 samples per atom) and accessible and non-accessible volume fractions (probe radius 1.3 Å). Chemical descriptors were elemental fractions, density, cell volume, atoms per cell, mean atomic number, mean and spread of Pauling electronegativity, and bonding-pattern counts per atom derived from a covalent-radius bond graph (two-coordinate N, N–H, NH_{{2}}, three-coordinate N, N–N bonds, O–H, C=O, ether O, boroxine and boronate-ester O, triazine C, and two-coordinate C). Each framework was assigned to a linkage class by rules applied in order to these counts (boroxine, boronate ester, β-ketoenamine, hydrazone, azine, triazine, imine, other). In total 37 descriptors were used. Heavy-tailed descriptors (cell volume, atoms per cell, surface area, pore volume) were log-transformed, and all descriptors were standardised to zero mean and unit variance before principal component analysis (PCA){cite("jolliffe2016")}. K-means clustering was carried out on the leading principal components that together explain 90% of the variance ({pca["ncomp90"]} components), and the framework closest to each of the k = 100 centroids was selected as a structurally diverse training pool.')
H2('Adsorption simulations')
P(f'Simulations used RASPA 2{cite("dubbeldam2016")} with rigid frameworks. Framework Lennard-Jones (LJ) parameters were taken from DREIDING for main-group elements{cite("mayo1990")} and from UFF for all other elements{cite("rappe1992")}, combined by Lorentz–Berthelot rules and truncated and shifted at 12 Å. Supercells were at least 24 Å wide in every perpendicular direction, and electrostatics were computed by Ewald summation (relative precision 10^{{−6}}). CO_{{2}} and N_{{2}} were described with the rigid TraPPE models{cite("potoff2001")}. Henry coefficients K_{{H}} and the average adsorption energy ⟨ΔU⟩ were obtained by Widom test-particle insertion at 298 K{cite("widom1963")} (1,500 cycles, about 15,000 insertions per gas). For the {int(st.refined.sum()) if "refined" in st else 133} frameworks with a relative uncertainty above 30% or in the top 60 by selectivity, the calculation was repeated with 6,000 cycles, and these values are reported. The isosteric heat at zero coverage was taken as Q_{{st}}^{{0}} = −⟨ΔU⟩ + RT, and the Henry selectivity as S = K_{{H}}(CO_{{2}})/K_{{H}}(N_{{2}}), which equals the adsorption selectivity of a binary mixture in the limit of zero loading{cite("myers1965")}. Uncertainties are block-average standard errors. For comparison with experiment, pure-CO_{{2}} grand-canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations (700 equilibration and 1,000 production cycles) were carried out at the experimental conditions, and excess uptakes were obtained using helium void fractions computed by Widom insertion of helium at 298 K.')
H2('Density functional theory')
P(f'Cluster models of five linkage motifs and a benzene reference were cut from representative experimental structures and capped with hydrogen atoms along the broken bonds (C–H 1.09 Å, N–H 1.01 Å). Heavy-atom positions were kept at their crystallographic values so that the models retain the geometry of the framework. One cluster (azine), whose deposited geometry contained bond lengths well outside typical ranges (N–N 1.33 Å, C–C 1.56 Å), was instead fully relaxed at the PBE-D3(BJ)/def2-SVP level. Calculations used PySCF 2.14{cite("sun2020")} with libxc{cite("lehtola2018")}, the PBE functional{cite("kohn1965", "perdew1996")} with Grimme’s D3 dispersion correction and Becke–Johnson damping{cite("grimme2010", "grimme2011")}, def2 basis sets{cite("weigend2005")} and Coulomb fitting (def2-universal-jfit){cite("weigend2006")}. The D3(BJ) two-body term was implemented from the reference parameters of the s-dftd3 library and reproduces its reference energies to better than 10^{{−10}} E_{{h}}. Candidate gas positions were generated by a force-field global search: 40,000 random poses of rigid CO_{{2}} or N_{{2}} around the cluster were scored with DREIDING/TraPPE parameters and electrostatic-potential-fitted cluster charges (PBE-D3(BJ)/def2-SVP), and the 30 lowest were minimised. Interaction energies at the global force-field minimum were then computed at the PBE-D3(BJ)/def2-TZVP level with the Boys–Bernardi counterpoise correction{cite("boys1970")}. To estimate the error from not relaxing the adsorbate at the DFT level, the CO_{{2}} complexes of benzene and the imine cluster were also fully relaxed (adsorbate free, cluster heavy atoms fixed) at PBE-D3(BJ)/def2-SVP with geomeTRIC{cite("wang2016")}.')
H2('Machine learning')
P(f'Models were trained to predict log_{{10}} S. The baselines were an RF (500 trees, minimum leaf size 2, one third of the features per split){cite("breiman2001")} and an MLP (two hidden layers of 128 and 64 units, L_{{2}} penalty 10^{{−3}}, early stopping), both in scikit-learn{cite("pedregosa2011")} and both using the 37 descriptors above. The GNN was SchNet{cite("schutt2018")} with three interaction blocks, 32 features, 16 Gaussian radial basis functions, a 4 Å cosine cut-off on periodic neighbour graphs and a mean (intensive) read-out with dropout (p = 0.1) before the output layer. It was trained with Adam{cite("kingma2015")} (learning rate 2 × 10^{{−3}}, halved after every eight epochs without improvement, at most 30 epochs, best epoch selected on a 10% validation split). Because no deep-learning framework could be installed in the compute environment, SchNet was implemented in NumPy with hand-written back-propagation and verified against finite-difference gradients (maximum relative error 2 × 10^{{−5}}). Performance was assessed by 5-fold cross-validation on all {n} frameworks with identical folds for every model. In the screening scenario, models were trained only on the diversity-selected frameworks that passed quality control ({ml["n_train_screen"]} of the 100) and evaluated on the other {ml["n_test_screen"]}. Uncertainty was estimated from the spread across trees (RF) and from an ensemble of three independently initialised networks plus Monte-Carlo dropout with 20 passes (SchNet){cite("lakshminarayanan2017", "gal2016")}. Feature attributions were computed as Shapley values{cite("lundberg2017")} estimated by Monte-Carlo permutation sampling{cite("strumbelj2014")} (150 frameworks, 12 permutations, 30 background samples).')

# ======================= RESULTS =======================
H1('Results')
H2('Chemical-space map and the origin of the original PCA artefact')
P(f'An earlier version of this analysis applied PCA to unscaled descriptors and found that the first component explained 99.8% of the variance. Repeating that calculation confirms the result (PC1 > 99.99%), but it is an artefact: the unit-cell volume (10^{{2}}–10^{{5}} Å^{{3}}) is several orders of magnitude larger than the elemental fractions (0–1), so it dominates the covariance matrix. After standardisation, variance is spread over many directions: PC1 and PC2 explain {pca["std_ev"][0]*100:.1f}% and {pca["std_ev"][1]*100:.1f}%, and {pca["ncomp90"]} components are needed to reach 90% (Fig. 1a). PC1 is dominated by density, cell volume and pore volume, PC2 by hydrogen content and cavity size, and PC3 by linkage chemistry (N–H, C=O, N–N). Silhouette scores of k-means partitions are low for every k (0.13–0.21; Fig. 1b), showing that the COF descriptor space is a continuum without natural clusters. The k = 100 partition is therefore used only as a diversity sampler, which spreads the training pool across the occupied region of chemical space (Fig. 1a, orange circles).')
FIG(f'{W}/figs/fig_pca.png', '**Figure 1 | Descriptor space of the CURATED-COFs frameworks.** **a**, Projection on the first two principal components of the standardised descriptors, coloured by simulated log_{10} S; orange circles mark the 100 frameworks closest to the k-means centroids. **b**, Silhouette score of k-means partitions as a function of k (dashed line: k = 100).', 2.8 / 7.2)
H2('CO_{2}/N_{2} selectivity of experimentally reported COFs')
P(f'Across the {n} frameworks the Henry selectivity at 298 K spans {f1(S.min())} to {S.max():.0f}, with a median of {f1(S.median())} and an interquartile range of {f1(S.quantile(0.25))}–{f1(S.quantile(0.75))} (Fig. 2a). The distribution is strongly right-skewed: {nS10} frameworks ({100*nS10/n:.0f}%) exceed S = 10 and {nS20} exceed S = 20. The zero-coverage CO_{{2}} adsorption enthalpy ranges from {f1(st.Qst_CO2.min())} to {f1(st.Qst_CO2.max())} kJ mol^{{−1}} (median {f1(st.Qst_CO2.median())} kJ mol^{{−1}}) and is almost perfectly rank-correlated with selectivity (ρ = {rho_q:.2f}; Fig. 2c). This is expected, because N_{{2}} binding varies much less than CO_{{2}} binding between frameworks, so selectivity is controlled by how strongly CO_{{2}} is held.')
P(f'Pore confinement is the single strongest structural correlate of selectivity. Selectivity decreases with the largest cavity diameter (ρ = {rho_lcd:.2f}; Fig. 2b): the median S is {f1(med_small)} for the {n_small} frameworks with LCD < 10 Å but only {f1(med_large)} for the {n_large} frameworks with LCD > 20 Å. In narrow pores a CO_{{2}} molecule interacts with several pore walls at once, and its quadrupole interacts with the framework charges at short range, whereas in mesoporous COFs most of the pore volume lies far from any wall. By contrast, the linkage class on its own is a weak predictor (Fig. 3a). Median selectivities of the major classes fall in a narrow window ({f1(lk.S_med.min())}–{f1(lk.S_med.max())}), and the spread within each class is far larger than the differences between classes.')
FIG(f'{W}/figs/fig2_landscape.png', f'**Figure 2 | CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} Henry selectivity of {n} experimentally reported COFs at 298 K.** **a**, Distribution (log scale; dashed line: median). **b**, Selectivity against the largest cavity diameter. **c**, Selectivity against the zero-coverage isosteric heat of CO_{{2}}.', 2.4 / 7.2)
top_rows = []
for _, r in top.iterrows():
    top_rows.append([r.Name, r.linkage, f'{r.S_henry:.0f} ± {r.S_henry_err:.0f}', f'{r.Qst_CO2:.1f}', f'{r.LCD:.1f}', f'{r.ASA_m2g:.0f}'])
P(f'The ten most selective frameworks are listed in Table 1. They combine narrow pores (LCD mostly below 15 Å) with polar or charged pore walls: metal-containing salen and phthalocyanine COFs, carboxylic-acid- and fluorine-decorated frameworks, and ultramicroporous 3D networks. For the most selective of these, the Widom estimates carry large statistical uncertainties even after 6,000 cycles, because the Boltzmann average is dominated by a small number of strongly binding configurations. These frameworks are therefore best viewed as candidates for higher-level follow-up (mixture GCMC with DFT-derived charges, or periodic DFT) rather than as definitive rankings.')
TAB('**Table 1 | The ten frameworks with the highest CO_{2}/N_{2} Henry selectivity at 298 K.** Uncertainties are block-average standard errors.',
    ['Framework', 'Linkage class', 'S (298 K)', 'Q_{st}^{0} (kJ mol^{−1})', 'LCD (Å)', 'ASA (m^{2} g^{−1})'], top_rows, [0.26, 0.18, 0.16, 0.16, 0.11, 0.13])
H2('Intrinsic binding of CO_{2} and N_{2} to linkage motifs')
rows = []
for m in dft['order']:
    d = dft['motifs'][m]
    rows.append([d['label'], d['source'], str(d['natoms']), f'{d["CO2"]:.1f}', f'{d["N2"]:.1f}', f'{d["CO2"]-d["N2"]:.1f}', f'{d["S_med"]:.1f}' if d.get('S_med') else '–'])
P(f'To separate chemical from geometric effects, we computed DFT interaction energies of CO_{{2}} and N_{{2}} with cluster models of five linkage motifs and a benzene reference (Table 2, Fig. 3b). At the PBE-D3(BJ)/def2-TZVP level with counterpoise correction, CO_{{2}} binds to the motifs with ΔE = {dft["co2_min"]:.1f} to {dft["co2_max"]:.1f} kJ mol^{{−1}}, compared with {dft["n2_min"]:.1f} to {dft["n2_max"]:.1f} kJ mol^{{−1}} for N_{{2}}. The CO_{{2}} preference ΔΔE = ΔE(CO_{{2}}) − ΔE(N_{{2}}) ranges from {dft["dd_min"]:.1f} to {dft["dd_max"]:.1f} kJ mol^{{−1}}. {dft["text_strongest"]} {dft["text_protocol"]}')
P(f'These single-site energies are of the same order as the spread in CO_{{2}} adsorption enthalpies between frameworks with similar pore sizes, but much smaller than the effect of confinement. A CO_{{2}} molecule in a 6–8 Å pore interacts with several walls, and the corresponding adsorption enthalpies (25–40 kJ mol^{{−1}}) exceed any single-motif binding energy in Table 2. The DFT results therefore support the conclusion from the screening that linkage chemistry tunes selectivity at the margin, whereas pore geometry sets its scale. They also indicate which motifs merit priority when designing narrow-pore COFs.')
TAB('**Table 2 | Counterpoise-corrected PBE-D3(BJ)/def2-TZVP interaction energies (kJ mol^{−1}) of CO_{2} and N_{2} with linkage-motif cluster models.** S_{med} is the median simulated Henry selectivity of the frameworks in the corresponding linkage class.',
    ['Motif', 'Cut from', 'Atoms', 'ΔE(CO_{2})', 'ΔE(N_{2})', 'ΔΔE', 'S_{med}'], rows, [0.22, 0.2, 0.09, 0.13, 0.13, 0.11, 0.12])
FIG(f'{W}/figs/fig3_linkage_dft.png', '**Figure 3 | Linkage chemistry.** **a**, Distribution of log_{10} S by linkage class (classes with at least ten frameworks; boxes: interquartile range; line: median). **b**, Counterpoise-corrected PBE-D3(BJ)/def2-TZVP interaction energies of CO_{2} and N_{2} with linkage-motif clusters.', 2.7 / 7.2)
H2('Machine-learning models')
schtxt = (f' SchNet, which sees only the atomic graph within a 4 Å cut-off, reaches MAE = {f3(sch["MAE"])}, R^{{2}} = {f2(sch["R2"])} and ρ = {f2(sch["spearman"])}.' if sch else '')
P(f'In 5-fold cross-validation (Table 3, Fig. 4a,b) the RF predicts log_{{10}} S with a mean absolute error (MAE) of {f3(rf["MAE"])} (a factor of {10**rf["MAE"]:.2f} in S), R^{{2}} = {f2(rf["R2"])} and Spearman ρ = {f2(rf["spearman"])}. The MLP trained on the same descriptors is clearly worse (MAE = {f3(mlp["MAE"])}, R^{{2}} = {f2(mlp["R2"])}).{schtxt} The same RF predicts Q_{{st}}^{{0}}(CO_{{2}}) with an MAE of {f1(rfq["MAE"])} kJ mol^{{−1}} (R^{{2}} = {f2(rfq["R2"])}).')
P(f'The weaker performance of the graph network probably reflects more than limited tuning. Selectivity in the Henry regime depends on the size and shape of the void and on how many walls a guest can contact. Neither quantity is local to any atom, and both are captured explicitly by Zeo++ descriptors but only indirectly by a message-passing network with a finite receptive field and a mean read-out over framework atoms. The Shapley analysis (Fig. 4d) confirms that pore geometry dominates the RF predictions: {shap_geo} carry most of the attribution, and the most important chemical descriptors ({shap_chem}) follow with much smaller weights. With about 800 labelled examples, a model given the right physical descriptors outperforms one that must learn them from atomic coordinates. Combining both representations is a natural next step.')
mlrows = [['Random forest (descriptors)', f3(rf['MAE']), f3(rf['RMSE']), f2(rf['R2']), f2(rf['spearman']), f3(rfs['MAE']), f2(rfs['spearman'])],
          ['MLP (descriptors)', f3(mlp['MAE']), f3(mlp['RMSE']), f2(mlp['R2']), f2(mlp['spearman']), f3(ml['MLP_screen']['MAE']), f2(ml['MLP_screen']['spearman'])]]
