H2('Comparison with experiment')
vrows = val['rows']
P(f'Simulated pure-CO_{{2}} excess uptakes at 298 K and 55 bar reproduce the experimental high-pressure capacities of the boron-based COFs reported by Furukawa and Yaghi{cite("furukawa2009")} with a mean absolute percentage deviation of {val["hp_mape"]:.0f}% (Table 4). At 273 K and 1 bar, COF-6 (simulated {val["cof6_sim"]:.0f} mg g^{{−1}}, experimental {val["cof6_exp"]:.0f} mg g^{{−1}}) is also reproduced well. In contrast, the uptake of the nitrogen-rich azine framework ACOF-1{cite("li2014acof")} is substantially underestimated ({val["acof_sim"]:.0f} versus 177 mg g^{{−1}}). The simulated Henry selectivity at 273 K ({val["acof_S"]:.1f}) and zero-coverage heat ({val["acof_Q"]:.1f} kJ mol^{{−1}}) are likewise below the reported initial-slope selectivity (40) and isosteric heat (27.6 kJ mol^{{−1}}).{val.get("jlu2_text","").replace("{c}", cite("li2015jlu2"))} High-pressure capacity is governed by pore volume and is therefore insensitive to the details of the binding sites. Low-pressure uptake in N-rich COFs, however, depends on the strongest sites, where generic LJ parameters, charge-equilibration charges and the neglect of polarisation, defects, residual solvent and stacking disorder all matter. The absolute selectivities reported here should therefore be read as a consistent ranking under one physical model, with the strongest-binding, N-rich candidates likely to be underestimated rather than overestimated.')
