# ======================= DISCUSSION =======================
H1('Discussion')
P(f'Three conclusions emerge. First, among experimentally reported COFs, CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} selectivity in the dilute limit is mainly a property of pore geometry. The median framework has S ≈ {S.median():.0f}, which is too low for practical DAC without chemisorptive functionalisation, and the frameworks that reach S > 20 are almost all ultramicroporous or carry strongly polar or charged groups. This is consistent with the design logic behind recent amine-functionalised COFs for DAC{cite("zhou2024")} and with earlier screening of hypothetical COFs{cite("deeg2020")}. The physisorptive COF landscape alone does not contain a DAC sorbent. It does, however, contain promising scaffolds, narrow-pore frameworks with modest intrinsic selectivity, onto which chemisorptive groups could be grafted.')
P(f'Second, for this task and data-set size a well-chosen set of physical descriptors outperforms an end-to-end graph network. This does not argue against GNNs in general: they excel when the target is a local electronic property or when tens of thousands of labels are available. For adsorption in porous frameworks, however, the void space is the relevant object, and it is not represented explicitly in an atom-centred graph. Hybrid models that feed pore descriptors into the read-out of a GNN, or graphs that include pore nodes, are promising directions. This mirrors observations in other data-scarce applications of machine learning, such as sensor-based disease diagnostics, where physics-informed and hybrid models outperform purely data-driven ones when labelled data are limited{cite("khanal2025qft")}.')
P(f'Third, the main value of ML here is triage, not replacement of simulation. A model trained on about 10% of the frameworks recovers most of the top performers after simulating a small fraction of the rest. Because each Widom calculation is inexpensive compared with periodic DFT, the gain is most valuable when the downstream calculation is costly, for example mixture GCMC with DFT-derived charges, or process-level evaluation of working capacity and parasitic energy{cite("huck2014", "farmahini2021")}.')
P(f'The study has limitations. Frameworks were treated as rigid, perfect crystals in their deposited geometries rather than DFT-optimised ones, which neglects flexibility, stacking disorder and defects. EQeq charges are known to be less accurate than density-derived charges and failed for a small number of frameworks. The Henry selectivity ignores competition at finite loading and the effect of water, which is decisive for flue gas and important for DAC{cite("boyd2019")}. The DFT cluster models capture single-site binding, not confinement, and their energies are single points at force-field-located geometries (the two relaxed benchmarks differ by less than 1 kJ mol^{{−1}}), and only one adsorption site per motif was examined. The SchNet model was trained with a limited budget (32 features, at most 30 epochs) imposed by CPU-only computation, so larger networks and longer training may narrow, although probably not close, the gap to the descriptor model. Finally, the experimental comparison is limited by the small number of COFs for which low-pressure CO_{{2}} isotherms and Henry-regime selectivities are reported under comparable conditions.')
H1('Conclusions')
P(f'We have mapped the CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} Henry selectivity and CO_{{2}} adsorption enthalpy of {n} experimentally reported COFs with a transparent, open workflow that combines Widom insertion, dispersion-corrected DFT on linkage motifs and machine learning. Pore confinement, not linkage chemistry, sets the scale of selectivity. Linkage motifs differ in intrinsic CO_{{2}} affinity by only a few kJ mol^{{−1}}, and a descriptor-based random forest predicts selectivity more accurately than a SchNet graph network trained on the same frameworks. Used as a triage tool, the random forest recovers most of the best frameworks after simulating a small fraction of the candidates. Simulated high-pressure capacities agree with experiment, whereas low-pressure uptake of N-rich frameworks is underestimated, which identifies the strongest binding sites as the main target for improved force fields. The ranked list, structures and models provided here are intended as a starting point for designing narrow-pore, functionalised COFs for direct air capture.')
